# Draft: kk: Implement VK_KHR_fragment_shader_barycentric
# https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/mesa/mesa/-/merge_requests/45585 state=opened
# ### What does this MR do and why?

<!--Describe in detail what your merge request does and why.-->

```
Implementation of VK_KHR_fragment_shader_barycentric for KosmicKrisp

Metal natively only covers the extension partially: [[barycentric_coord]] works
only at the pixel center and also only once per shader and additionally per-vertex
values (vertex_value<T>) are only listed for M5 and later. Therefore we have two paths:

- Native: Barycentric at the pixel center uses [[barycentric_coord]] directly with
  no extra costs.
- Emulated: PerVertexKHR, centroid/sample/offset, mixed kinds and strips/fans.
  Emulated draws become non-indexed lists with one vertex shader invocation per primitive
  corner. The costs are about 1.5x on a dense test mesh.

Side effect: The triangle-merge workaround now also runs for any off-center interpolation
or sample shading, i.e. also for MSAA rendering without barycentric coordinates.

Not handled are adjacency topologies. They require geometryShader, which KosmicKrisp
doesn't expose yet, but the barycentric CTS runs 1,488 tests with them anyway. In my point
of view this seems to be a CTS bug.

Testing:
- dEQP-VK.fragment_shading_barycentric.*: 2,581 pass, 1,488 crash (all adjacency).
- Full vk-default against main:
  - There are no regressions
  - A few changed tests outside this group seem to be flaky and pass on rerun.
- Locally I've added a small test app that covers what the CTS doesn't
  (indirect/indirect count, multi-draw, multiview, secondary command buffers, robust
  index reads, pipeline caches, primitive restart) and also cross-tested it with an NVIDIA GPU.

cc @aitor
